        "# ML-03 — Frame Your Lane as an ML Task\n",
        "\n",
        "This skeleton is yours to fill. Work the sections **in order** — each one has a one-line hint. Simple words, honest numbers.\n",
        "\n",
        "> Working with an AI assistant? Tell it to read `skills/README.md` first and load the one skill this assignment names on its card."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 1. My lane as an ML task (type)\n",
        "\n",
        "*Classification, clustering, ranking, or scoring — which one, and why?*\n",
        "\n",
        "**Lane 2: Refresh / Content Opportunity Scoring** is best framed as a scoring/ranking task.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Why:** The Editorial capacity is strictly limited and can only do a set of instructions on a limited number of pages per week. Scoring and ranking outputs a continuous opportunity score that orders the site catalog into a prioritized action queue with clear reason codes, ensuring editors maximize recoverable search traffic per hour spent."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "\n",
        "# Load starter dataset slice to verify catalog dimensions for ranking setup\n",
        "df = pd.read_csv(\"data/raw/content_refresh_anonymized.csv\")\n",
        "print(f\"Total catalog pages loaded: {len(df)}\")\n",
        "print(\"Task Choice: Scoring / Ranking"\)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 2. Target or proxy\n",
        "\n",
        "*What would you predict? Where does that label come from — observed outcome or a defined rule?*\n",
        "\n",
        "**Predicted Target:** Proxy Label — Absolute Recoverable Organic Traffic Loss / Performance Decay ($\n",
        "\\Delta \\text{Clicks}$) observed over a subsequent evaluation window.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Label Source:** **Observed outcome** derived from historical daily performance log data (e.g., measuring actual click/impression changes between a baseline window and a subsequent evaluation period). The label represents measured real-world traffic loss, not an arbitrary manual threshold or defined heuristic rule."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Code check: Summarize observed performance features available for target proxy formulation\n",
        "print(\"Observed Performance Signals Summary:\")\n",
        "summary_cols = [c for c in [\"word_count\", \"ai_sessions_90d\", \"ai_traffic_pct\"] if c in df.columns]\n",
        "print(df[summary_cols].describe().T[['mean', 'std', 'min', '50%', 'max']])"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 3. Success metric\n",
        "\n",
        "*One metric you can defend. What number means 'good'?*\n",
        "\n",
        "**Primary Success Metric:** **Precision@20** (supplemented by NDCG@50 across the full ranked action queue).\n",
        "\n",
        "**What number means 'good':**\n",
        "- **Precision@20 $\\ge$ 0.75**: At least 15 out of the top 20 pages surfaced in the action queue represent genuine refresh or content opportunities upon manual editorial review.\n",
        "- **Baseline Benchmark:** Achieves a **+15% improvement in Precision@20** over a baseline heuristic rule (e.g., sorting purely by raw page age or thin word count)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Demonstration: Evaluating Precision@K framework for human-in-the-loop review\n",
        "def evaluate_top_k_queue(df, score_col, k=20):\n",
        "    top_k = df.sort_values(by=score_col, ascending=False).head(k)\n",
        "    return top_k\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Precision@20 evaluation framework initialized for manual top-20 review queue.\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 4. The unit of analysis, as a real dataframe\n",
        "\n",
        "*Load your lane's slice and show it: one row = one what?*\n",
        "\n",
        "**Unit of Analysis:** One row = **One content page (`content_id`) evaluated within a specific snapshot window.**\n",
        "\n",
        "The dataframe slice below shows page attributes (`content_type`, `main_intent`, `word_count`) alongside performance signals used to generate opportunity scores, action suggestions, and reason codes."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "\n",
        "df = pd.read_csv(\"data/raw/content_refresh_anonymized.csv\")\n",
        "\n",
        "content_slice = df[[\n",
        "    \"content_id\", \"client_id\", \"content_type\", \"main_intent\",\n",
        "    \"word_count\", \"ai_sessions_90d\", \"ai_traffic_pct\"\n",
        "]]\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"One row = one content page\")\n",
        "content_slice.head(10)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 5. Why ML beats a fixed rule here\n",
        "\n",
        "*What makes the pattern too messy for an if-statement?*\n",
        "\n",
        "Static rules like `if word_count < 1000 or click_drop > 20% then refresh` fail in production for three reasons:\n",
        "1. **Seasonality & Macro Noise:** Fixed rules flag seasonal traffic dips as content decay, wasting editorial time on pages that will naturally bounce back.\n",
        "2. **Non-Linear Value Trade-offs:** A 5% traffic drop on a high-volume pillar page usually represents far higher recoverable value than an 80% drop on a low-traffic niche page.\n",
        "3. **Action Disambiguation:** Static logic cannot cleanly assign specific action categories (*refresh*, *expand*, *protect*, *prune*, *monitor*) or output calibrated reason codes across multi-variable signals."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Demonstration: Failure mode of a fixed heuristic rule\n",
        "rule_matches = df[(df[\"word_count\"] < 1500) & (df[\"main_intent\"] == \"informational\")]\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Total pages matched by static rule: {len(rule_matches)}\")\n",
        "print(\"Conclusion: Static rules produce uncalibrated output queue sizes without clear priority ranking or action reason codes.\")"
      ]
    },
